00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.150
JOEL HEGG: All right, so
the vision for eBay ShopBot

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.430
was an intelligent and
trusted personal shop

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.500
assistant that
understands your intent

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.780
and shops for and with you.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.530
This video showed a
multi-turn, conversational AI

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:37.170
that was simple, fast,
and conversational.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.500
Our AI demonstrates natural
language processing, computer

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.950
vision, and speech recognition.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.720
It curates and highlights
the best deals for you

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.540
and shows the unique finds.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.410
We have a size
profile that allows

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.640
us to remember your
preferences, and as you continue

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.820
to shop with us, we'll
remember what you entered

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.510
and personalize your
shopping experience for you.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.640
And then, of course,
the end result

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.962
is an item card, where we
take you to an eBay page

00:05:12.962 --> 00:05:15.170
where you can purchase the
item that you shopped for.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.380
A major aspect of what
we've done here is ubiquity.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.460
Instead of expecting
users to come

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:30.050
to ebay.com or our mobile
app, we go to the channels

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.100
where they already are.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.933
If they're in Google
Assistant or if they're

00:05:32.933 --> 00:05:34.580
in Facebook Messenger,
we want them

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.760
to be able to shop
from that interface.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.830
We adjust conversations
based on the capabilities

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.680
of the channel and device
the user is currently in.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.710
So if you're in your kitchen
and you have a Google Home,

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:50.630
you should be able to shop using
your voice from your kitchen.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:54.110
We allow users to interact
seamlessly across devices,

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:55.730
resuming where they left off.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:06:00.016
So going back to that example
of using your Google Home.

00:06:00.016 --> 00:06:02.390
At some point, you might want
to actually see the results

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.094
that we provided for you.

00:06:04.094 --> 00:06:06.260
So we can transfer the
conversation from your Google

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.685
Home to Google Assistant on
your phone for you to visualize.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.280
So while ubiquity
was our goal, we

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.330
decided to start with
two markets first.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.680
One is Google Assistant, which
has over 500 million devices

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.100
today, and Facebook
Messenger, which has

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.980
over 1.3 billion monthly users.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:33.150
On Google Assistant, you
create an action to personalize

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.530
the interface as a developer.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:38.230
They have over 1
million actions today.

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:45.700
On Facebook Messenger,
there are over 300,000 bots.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.820
So now, I'll jump into
the technical details.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.920
I'll explain what a
conversational platform

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:53.460
normally looks like and how
you would interact with it,

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.339
and then I'll talk about
why we didn't do it

00:06:55.339 --> 00:06:56.130
in the typical way.

00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:00.690
So, for any conversational
platform you integrate with,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:04.980
this is kind of the
diagram that you

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.340
would want to build towards.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:11.345
On the left, you have the user's
input device, like their phone.

00:07:11.345 --> 00:07:14.585
It could be Google Assistant,
where they might input speech.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.470
It could be Facebook Messenger,
where you upload an image.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.890
And then, that's received by
the conversational platform.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.310
That's converted
into a request that's

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:30.599
sent to the custom WebHook
that you create as a developer.

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:33.140
You would, of course, want to
validate that that request came

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.810
from the platform.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.790
Then, you would
formulate a response

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.880
and then send back the response
synchronously or asynchronously

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:42.134
to the platform.

00:07:42.134 --> 00:07:43.550
In the case of
Facebook Messenger,

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.607
they have a model that's
completely asynchronous.

00:07:46.607 --> 00:07:48.440
With Google Assistant,
you have five seconds

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:51.980
to respond to the
incoming request.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.410
And then, finally, when
the conversational platform

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.160
receives the response
from your WebHook,

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.060
it routes the
output to the user.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.200
So this is Google's
suggested variant

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.080
of this approach for
Google Assistant.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.070
Similar to what happened
in the last diagram,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.930
you have the input
device on the left.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.430
On the right, you
have your WebHook,

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.660
which they use Cloud Functions
for in all their samples.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.670
Actions on Google is the
name of their platform

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.260
that you would develop against.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.130
And what's different
here is in the middle,

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.640
they have Dialogflow, which is
a natural language processing

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.900
tool that they've built.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.700
In this example, the
user might say, ask eBay

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.350
what my iPhone is worth.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:36.770
This will be send from Actions
on Google to Dialogflow.

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.900
It will do the natural
language processing

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.330
and determine that the
intent of this request

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:46.070
was a what it's worth
request and the parameters

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:54.290
included a product parameter
with the value of iPhone X.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:57.170
So we actually did start with
Dialogflow for Google Home.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:59.510
We found it to be pretty
good for rapid prototyping,

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:02.750
and it helps you get going
in just a few minutes

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.410
with a working integration.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.900
We built what I just
described in the last page,

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.686
or what its workflow,
in about five days.

00:09:11.686 --> 00:09:13.060
And we actually
were able to demo

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.960
at the keynote at this
conference last year.

00:09:19.286 --> 00:09:21.160
But as we went further
on in the integration,

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:25.390
we felt like we kind of outgrew
that workflow at some point.

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:27.560
The first issue was it was
challenging to scale up

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:29.260
development for our team.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:33.970
Their tool is a kind of a
point-and-click website,

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:38.560
and it didn't, at the time, have
environment support or revision

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.960
control, all the kind of tools
that you, as a developer,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.610
are normally used to having.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:45.077
And so we had kind of
a medium-sized team,

00:09:45.077 --> 00:09:46.660
and beyond a few
developers, it became

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:50.079
kind of challenging
to work with this.

00:09:50.079 --> 00:09:52.120
There is a limited set of
conversational platform

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:53.170
integrations.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.000
So going back to
our original goal

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:59.170
of being ubiquitous, what
you see there on the right

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.770
is the logos from their
website of all the platforms

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:02.454
they support.

00:10:02.454 --> 00:10:04.870
And there are actually a lot
more conversational platforms

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.540
in the world than that.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:13.060
Our original integration was
based on a few key categories

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.525
where we imported our
catalog into Dialogflow

00:10:15.525 --> 00:10:16.900
so it could do
the national image

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:20.470
processing based on our items.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:24.139
In reality, our entire catalog
has 1.1 billion listings.

00:10:24.139 --> 00:10:25.930
It was just way too
much to actually import

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:27.566
into their systems.

00:10:27.566 --> 00:10:28.940
And then we had
a requirement for

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:33.330
a dynamic, multi-turn dialog.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:39.160
Dialogflow is kind of
built for more static kind

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:44.670
of closed,
retrieval-based systems.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.800
And then, finally,
we are a big company.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.670
We actually do have
in-house AI expertise,

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.170
and so we didn't
feel like we needed

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:52.640
kind of a more basic tool.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.470
We wanted to be able
to do something more

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.220
advanced with our AI and NLU.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.650
So not to pick on
Dialogflow, but finding

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.440
the right off-the-shelf
platform in this space

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:08.390
is an ongoing struggle.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:10.490
Bot frameworks are in a
constant state of flux.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.410
So similar to
Dialogflow, there's

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:16.130
a product called Wit.ai
that was bought by Facebook.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.470
And now, if you go
to their website,

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.994
it's almost all geared
towards Facebook Messenger.

00:11:21.994 --> 00:11:24.660
It's not really considered to be
a general purpose tool anymore,

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.080
even though you probably
could use it that way.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:30.360
Vendor lock-in and
upselling are common.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.200
So if you were to look
at Skype a few years ago,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:34.970
they had a bot API.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.390
And if you integrated with it,
within a little bit of time,

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:42.144
you had to upgrade to the
Microsoft bot framework.

00:11:42.144 --> 00:11:43.560
That went through
a few revisions.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.680
Now it's called the
Azure Bot Service,

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.380
and that's been used to also
upsell people on Microsoft

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.990
Cognitive Services.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:54.076
So you might do a
simple free integration,

00:11:54.076 --> 00:11:55.950
and within a couple
years, you might actually

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:59.720
have to pay real money
to keep it alive.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:02.710
So this has driven us, over
time, to build abstractions

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.870
over these
conversational platforms

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:08.710
and abstract away the
platform-specific details

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:11.380
into just a kind
of closed area so

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:16.440
that we're not impacted by this
flux from these third parties.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:23.950
And then, the other part
of the original diagram

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:27.260
I showed that Google recommended
was the use of Cloud Functions.

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.230
So when we originally
looked at this,

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:30.670
we ran into a few
issues that made

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:32.170
it kind of a non-option for us.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.520
One were language limitations.

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:39.250
Our WebHook was written in
Scala, and it required Node.js.

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:44.950
There was an issue with not
having raw requests access.

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:47.380
For Facebook Messenger,
the entire security story

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.700
is based on getting
the raw requests,

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.950
computing the hash on it,
comparing it to a header

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:53.660
that they send you.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:58.714
Without that support, they were
parsing the JSON, stripping out

00:12:58.714 --> 00:13:00.380
whitespace, and you
could never get back

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.830
to the original request
and actually validate

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:05.150
that it was secure.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:07.160
There was no SLA,
so we saw issues

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:12.720
with uneven response
times when we were

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.640
trying to use Cloud Functions.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.264
And then there was no virtual
private cloud support,

00:13:17.264 --> 00:13:18.930
which was an issue
for us, because we're

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:21.300
using Kubernetes with VPC.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:25.860
And in order to access
our back-end services,

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.410
we need to be able to
get through the VPC.

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:32.130
Otherwise, we'd have to
expose our services publicly.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.950
So this was my site I
had up until last night,

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:38.751
when I read the news that
Cloud Function's now GA,

00:13:38.751 --> 00:13:40.750
and they pretty much
addressed all these issues.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.680
So we might actually
need to re-evaluate it.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.770
One, they added Python
support for languages.

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:49.027
They added container support.

00:13:49.027 --> 00:13:50.610
So I don't know what
that means, but I

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:54.870
think it means that you can
have a normal Docker image

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.240
and run that inside
Cloud Functions.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:58.980
They have raw
requests access now.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:00.330
They have SLA.

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:01.970
They have VPC support.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:05.460
They even allow you to run
Cloud Functions inside your GKE

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:06.870
environment.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:08.738
So definitely worth
re-evaluating.

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.080
So let me dig into our approach.

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:19.569
This is our architecture
at a high level.

00:14:19.569 --> 00:14:21.360
And I'll briefly go
over each of the areas,

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:24.020
but I'll dig into them later.

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:27.200
On the left, we have
a user's input device.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:29.570
They might interact with
actions on Google or Facebook

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:31.160
Messenger.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:34.010
The request is
sent to our WebHook

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:37.600
through a Google
global load balancer.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:39.860
We'll immediately try
and identify the user

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:43.250
by looking up in our own
internal identity service.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:44.910
From there, we'll
send the request

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:50.090
to our AI subsystem, which
will process the request

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.370
and generate a response.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:56.510
Along the way, we'll
use reliable messaging

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.420
that we built to make sure
that the request is handled

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:02.240
and the response is delivered.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.030
We record the requested
response in our chat history,

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:08.680
and we also do tracking
through Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:11.990
If the user uploaded a
media, like an image,

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:15.540
we'll write the media
to Cloud Storage.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.915
And we also have a service
for doing notifications

00:15:17.915 --> 00:15:20.240
so that we can send
requests or send messages

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:22.778
to the user proactively.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:28.050
So this is our AI
subsystem, which is

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:30.780
the brains behind eBay ShopBot.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:35.797
We have a custom NLU for
understanding language.

00:15:35.797 --> 00:15:37.380
We have a Knowledge
Graph, which helps

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:39.463
us understand the relationships
between the things

00:15:39.463 --> 00:15:41.580
the user is asking about.

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:45.540
We have a dialog manager, which
takes the output of those two

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.850
services and combines
it with the content

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:51.180
that we provide to formulate
the response for the user.

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.010
And then this
dialog manager will

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.410
use an AI memory to
remember what the user said

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:56.790
between conversational turns.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:04.365
So, going into our natural
language understanding area

00:16:04.365 --> 00:16:07.530
a little more, it transforms
natural language text

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.994
into a formal, machine-readable
representation.

00:16:10.994 --> 00:16:11.910
So it handles parsing.

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.639
It'll take a string,
break it up into words,

00:16:14.639 --> 00:16:16.680
identify nouns and verbs,
and add other structure

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.680
to the original input text.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.870
It does intent
detection to determine

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.480
what was the key thing
the user is trying to do.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.152
And then it extracts
any entities

00:16:26.152 --> 00:16:27.360
that it can find in the text.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:31.090
So, basically, parameters
to the original intent.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:34.200
So the example I have
here is, my husband

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.330
needs some new black
leather dress shoes,

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.940
but I want to spend
less than $80.

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.410
What do you have?

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:42.150
So our NLU will go
through this, and it

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.233
does a bunch of things I
won't go into completely.

00:16:44.233 --> 00:16:47.250
But the key thing is it
identifies the intent.

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:49.579
So needs and what
do you have tells it

00:16:49.579 --> 00:16:50.870
that this is a shopping intent.

00:16:53.475 --> 00:16:55.890
It identifies, also,
entities, including

00:16:55.890 --> 00:17:00.790
gender, item condition, color,
product, and price range.

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:02.910
And then, what you
see there on the top

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:04.560
is it did spelling correction.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.310
So husband was
spelled incorrectly,

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:07.809
so it corrected
that before it tried

00:17:07.809 --> 00:17:09.869
to do the entity extraction.

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:11.825
This is something
we've had for a while.

00:17:11.825 --> 00:17:13.283
Dialogflow, I think,
just announced

00:17:13.283 --> 00:17:16.050
that they added support
for this yesterday.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:20.260
And then, on the right, we have
some basic sentiment analysis

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:20.760
that we do.

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:25.260
So that emoji tells us something
about the user's state of mind,

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:30.150
and we can possibly tailor
the response based on that.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:31.550
This runs in Kubernetes.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.370
We have a variety of
machine-learned models

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:36.680
that we store in GCS.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:38.270
And when the machine
starts up, it

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:41.360
loads them into persistent
SSD-backed volumes.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:49.060
We have a Knowledge Graph that
is a probabilistic inference

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:52.580
graph of eBay user
behavioral data.

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.640
It allows us to infer
a shopping funnel,

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:58.090
so if the user said shoes, we
can narrow down the aspects

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:02.450
to just things that
are relevant to shoes.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:06.580
It runs with Apache
Airflow to pull data out

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:13.010
of eBay, Wikidata,
Wikipedia, and that

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:15.370
is handled by Cloud Dataproc--

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:19.240
which is Google's Spark
implementation-- and loads this

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:23.280
into a Neo4j graph database.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.830
At this point, we have
over 500 million nodes

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.530
and 20 billion edges.

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:33.210
So it requires a large amount
of CPU in memory to run this.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.790
But we also use persistent
SST-backed volumes, as well.

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:38.640
And we use staple sets
so that as we deploy,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.460
it doesn't have to reload all
that data onto the machine.

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:50.730
So this is an example of what
our Knowledge Graph looks like,

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:53.670
just so you can
understand more about it.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:56.802
On the left, let's say
the user said shoes.

00:18:56.802 --> 00:18:58.260
We know, usually,
what their gender

00:18:58.260 --> 00:18:59.490
is, especially on
Facebook messenger,

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:00.948
where they have a
profile where you

00:19:00.948 --> 00:19:04.180
can load information some basic
information about the user.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:07.440
So we can skip right to the
next level question, which

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.690
in the case of a male, this
graph is saying that there

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.240
is a higher chance that
he cares about color

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.310
as his next question than brand.

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:16.500
So we can ask him, which
color do you prefer--

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.920
red or blue?

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:22.490
If it's a female, we would go
right to the brand question

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:25.130
and ask, do you
prefer Coach or Aldo?

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:36.720
Our AI memory maintains memory
across conversational turns.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.060
It is our dialogue
state, and it also

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:42.710
keeps track of expected
future responses

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:46.040
if we are expecting more
input from the user.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:49.450
So going back to the flux
that you see sometimes

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:51.659
when you're dealing with
conversational platforms,

00:19:51.659 --> 00:19:53.200
this is one area
where you might want

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:56.140
to build your own AI memory.

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:58.630
Google's system provides a
dialog state object that you

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:00.730
can use on their system.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:03.310
Something like Microsoft
Bot Framework had this,

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:04.630
and they took it away.

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:07.090
So if you're going to integrate
with something like Cortana

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.100
through Microsoft
Bot Framework, you'd

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:10.910
have to build this
sort of system anyway.

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:13.970
So you might as well
use it everywhere.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:16.480
It uses Big Table, and it's
keyed on a conversation

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:20.600
ID, which is the ID that
the conversational platform

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:27.600
provides to us for each
turn of the conversation.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:30.230
And then what we store
is the serialized version

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:33.210
of our dialog manager's
in-memory context.

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:38.240
So as we go through each
turn of the conversation,

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:44.064
we will load this context out of
our AI memory, we'll update it,

00:20:44.064 --> 00:20:45.980
and then we'll write it
back out to Big Table.

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.110
Our dialog manager
manages the dialog flows.

00:20:55.582 --> 00:20:57.040
The content behind
it is maintained

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:59.140
by editors and
designers, and this

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.760
is the voice of eBay ShopBot.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:05.920
It leverages and
updates our AI memory.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.400
And it looks at the NLU output,
AI memory, and the Knowledge

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:11.930
Graph to generate
further responses.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.230
So this can lead to
another question,

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:15.730
or it can produce the results.

00:21:20.260 --> 00:21:24.300
So I'm going to jump into
our WebHook and AI subsystem

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:25.281
interaction.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:31.950
So our conversational
WebHook is built

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:35.302
in Node.js express TypeScript.

00:21:37.954 --> 00:21:39.970
It currently lives in
Kubernetes, as I said,

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:42.260
but we could possibly port
this to Cloud Functions

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:43.790
in the future.

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:49.040
We built that as a single
service with one router,

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.840
essentially, per
conversational platform

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:52.190
that we integrate with.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:54.290
We could split into
separate microservices

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:56.180
later on if we wanted to.

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:58.070
And the philosophy is
for this to basically

00:21:58.070 --> 00:21:59.840
be a translation
mechanism between

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:02.210
the third-party
conversational platform

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.420
and our own internal
request response format

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:06.440
that we call Fabric API.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:11.980
So this is what it looks like.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:14.970
It's our internal model
of a conversational event.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:22.350
And we call it Fabric because
we picture our experience being

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:23.910
on an ecosystem of
channels that we

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:26.670
want the bot to weave through.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:31.260
We recently built this as a
REST JSON base interaction,

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:37.460
but we wanted to have a concrete
interface to build against.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:39.270
We also wanted to
have client generation

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.760
for the various languages
that we integrate with.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:45.620
So we defined this interface
using protocol buffers.

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:49.790
And I'll go into the areas
there, which are basically

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:54.590
a subset of the things that we
built for this, for the fabric

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:56.000
interface.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:02.816
Event is the top-level
type, and it encapsulates

00:23:02.816 --> 00:23:04.940
a lot of things you would
see across conversational

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.580
platforms-- so
sender, recipient,

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:09.890
conversation for
maintaining context

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:11.960
across conversational turns.

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:15.879
We encode capabilities that
might come from the request.

00:23:15.879 --> 00:23:17.420
And then we break
down the event type

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:21.140
into either a
message, a post back,

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:23.990
which is essentially
a button click.

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:27.080
And then we can differentiate
between new users

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.240
and returning users
who are starting

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:32.280
a new conversation with us.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:37.360
Our message type allows us to
handle both speech and text.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:39.770
It allows the user
to upload media,

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:45.190
including images,
audio, video, and files.

00:23:45.190 --> 00:23:48.880
It has a schedule attribute that
allows us to basically delay

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:52.450
messages so that we can kind
of provide pauses in the user

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:53.780
interaction.

00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:56.350
We also have a typing
indicator in there,

00:23:56.350 --> 00:24:00.730
as well, that makes it look
like the bot is thinking.

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:03.760
We have a card type that's
kind of a generic card

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:07.390
that we can use to
fill in with any items,

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:11.260
but usually we use it
for an eBay product.

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:14.650
So we put in there the title, a
price, a description, an image,

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:17.890
and buttons that you'd
use to interact with it.

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:19.570
Going back to the
message, we have

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:24.490
a carousel, which is a
horizontally scrolling

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:25.305
set of cards.

00:24:25.305 --> 00:24:26.680
And we have a
ListTemplate, which

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:29.050
is a vertical version
of the same thing.

00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:37.500
So one of the key things here
is modeling channel and device

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:38.970
capabilities.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.100
So we want to able to
adapt the responses

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:43.210
to the channel and device.

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:46.030
So we have static config
based on the channel.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:48.570
So, for example, what
you see here on the right

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:52.620
is Google Assistant
showing a vertical list.

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:55.050
On Google Assistant, this
can handle up to 30 items.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:56.799
On Facebook Messenger,
their vertical list

00:24:56.799 --> 00:24:57.750
can handle four items.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:24:59.250
So when we're
integrating with this,

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:02.460
we want to be able to factor
in the different capabilities

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.710
of the different channels.

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:07.290
We also have dynamic config
based on the incoming request.

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.498
And this is especially
important on Google Assistant,

00:25:09.498 --> 00:25:12.760
where you can have a Google
Home that's purely voice based.

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:15.000
Or you can have a Google
Assistant on your phone

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:17.610
that has a screen interaction.

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:22.080
So we need to be able to factor
in the two types of voice

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:24.520
and screen-based interactions.

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:26.310
NVIDIA Shield is
one product that

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:28.610
has Google Assistant
running on it,

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:30.690
but they didn't add
a web browser yet,

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:34.867
so we can have the user linked
to ebay.com, for example.

00:25:34.867 --> 00:25:37.200
So we need to factor in all
these different capabilities

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:41.012
when we format our response.

00:25:41.012 --> 00:25:42.720
When we do that,
though, we want to avoid

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:45.840
having channel-specific
logic, because we're

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:48.000
trying to create a
bot that has kind

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.340
of a consistent voice across
these different channels.

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:54.170
We want to be able to reuse that
content across those channels

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:56.280
without having logic
that says if Messenger

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:58.609
or if Actions on Google.

00:25:58.609 --> 00:26:00.150
We want to be able
to say, basically,

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:01.350
in terms of
capabilities, this is

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:03.433
what you would show that
would optimize experience

00:26:03.433 --> 00:26:04.770
for this device the user has.

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:09.960
So just to kind of
drive that home,

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:13.450
this is an example of what that
looks like on Actions on Google

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.570
and Facebook Messenger.

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:20.090
These are the basic request
payloads that you would get.

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:23.110
In both cases, the
user said, hello world.

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:27.694
And we would translate that
into a single Fabric request,

00:26:27.694 --> 00:26:29.610
and it looks almost
identical between the two.

00:26:33.272 --> 00:26:36.060
We include the user ID, which
is our internal user ID.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:41.010
We record the channel ID, and
we take the conversation ID

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:43.770
that the conversational
platform gave us

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:45.180
and add that here, as well.

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:46.300
We add capabilities.

00:26:46.300 --> 00:26:47.925
And then, finally,
in our message list,

00:26:47.925 --> 00:26:52.340
we include the original
hello world text message.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:55.890
The response is pretty
much the same thing.

00:26:55.890 --> 00:27:00.130
In this case, our AI responded
with, you said, hello world.

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:03.780
And we have channel-specific
code in our WebHook hook

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:07.650
that can translate the Fabric
response into either Actions

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:08.984
on Google or Facebook Messenger.

00:27:08.984 --> 00:27:11.233
And what you see there is
what that looks like on each

00:27:11.233 --> 00:27:12.129
of those platforms.

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.587
So at this point, supporting
additional channels

00:27:19.587 --> 00:27:21.170
has become more of
a business decision

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:22.970
than an engineering one.

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.590
We have a common back end
shared by all channels.

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:28.880
And we just have to create
new express router in our app

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:33.429
and set the endpoint for that
in our compositional platform.

00:27:33.429 --> 00:27:35.845
The biggest challenge is then
mapping the external request

00:27:35.845 --> 00:27:38.370
and response format
into our Fabric API

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:40.120
and accounting for the
unique capabilities

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:42.467
that that channel might have.

00:27:42.467 --> 00:27:44.050
We've already validated
this approach.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:47.260
So beyond Google
Assistant and Messenger,

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:53.214
we've done this integration with
Cortana and Skype, SMS, Slack.

00:27:53.214 --> 00:27:54.880
And we've gotten
something basic working

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.220
that we know we could easily
scale out if we chose to take

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:58.526
our business in that direction.

00:28:03.786 --> 00:28:05.910
So I'll go into some of
the supporting systems now.

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:08.610
These are not major
systems or major components

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:10.260
of what we built.
But I think it's

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:15.720
interesting to look at them,
because they were built heavily

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:17.172
leveraging GCP.

00:28:17.172 --> 00:28:18.630
And it's the sort
of thing it would

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:22.280
take a lot more work
to build off GCP,

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:23.760
like on a traditional platform.

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:29.910
So we built our chat
history in Big Table,

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:33.016
and we want to archive
incoming and outgoing messages.

00:28:33.016 --> 00:28:34.890
So some use cases include
internal dashboards

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:40.320
to visualize conversations,
analytics, retraining our AI

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:45.110
models, and hand over
to the human operator.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:47.300
We basically are
just trying to record

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:49.920
a JSON serialized
version of the message.

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:51.930
We include some metadata.

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:54.020
We have a version attribute
that allows us to know

00:28:54.020 --> 00:28:56.150
how to parse that message.

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:59.060
We include the channel,
and then we include a send

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:00.830
from user value
to basically know

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:04.640
if it was a request or response
that we're recording here.

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:07.040
The key thing here, if you
spend any time at Big Table,

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:09.800
is on the key design.

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:12.650
And this kind of aligns with
Big Table's best practice

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.140
for how to build a key.

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:18.670
But we want to be able to
retrieve based on a user ID,

00:29:18.670 --> 00:29:20.910
so that's the first
component of our key.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:24.150
Then, we also want to be able to
make sure that the messages are

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:28.470
in order according to the
time that the user interacted

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:29.910
with our bot.

00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:31.500
So we use a timestamp.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:34.290
We wanted to be able to get
those most recent first,

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:37.480
so we reverse the timestamp.

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:39.460
And then we use a
random number, in case

00:29:39.460 --> 00:29:41.080
there are multiple
concurrent writers.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:43.400
We don't want them to
overwrite each other.

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:46.210
And then if we're recording
multiple messages at once,

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:49.436
we have a batch ID that's
sequential for that batch.

00:29:49.436 --> 00:29:51.310
So it all kind of comes
together and gives us

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:53.850
one, easy to interact with,
lightweight chat history

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:54.350
system.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:01.650
We actually built a reliable
busing system in Big Table.

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:03.690
So the thing here is
we want to make sure

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:07.060
that every input from
the user gets a response.

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:10.680
If something goes wrong in our
back end and we drop a request,

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:11.656
it looks really bad.

00:30:11.656 --> 00:30:13.530
It essentially looks
like the bot is just not

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:14.446
functioning very well.

00:30:14.446 --> 00:30:17.370
It's low quality.

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:19.590
It also aligns with Facebook
Messenger's interaction

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:23.370
with WebHooks, which is
they'll send you a request,

00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:26.310
and they expect you to
return a 200 response.

00:30:26.310 --> 00:30:29.910
And if you don't, they'll
retry it again in 20 seconds.

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:31.650
If you return too
many failures, they'll

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:33.870
disable your bot altogether.

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:35.400
So no matter what
happens, we want

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:36.750
to be able to return a 200.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:41.500
So the system we built takes
a request from Messenger,

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:44.850
and we immediately
write it to Big Table.

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:47.700
Each user has their own
queue, which is represented

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:50.550
by a row in Big Table.

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:52.980
And the message is
written to a column

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:57.810
with a sequential
monotonically increasing ID.

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:00.460
We'll try and send the
message, if we can, right away.

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:05.400
But if something goes wrong,
a worker will scan the table.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:10.010
It will pick up the row that
still has outstanding messages.

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:12.600
It will automatically
try and lease the row,

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.670
and then it'll send the
messages one by one.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:16.650
After all the
columns are deleted,

00:31:16.650 --> 00:31:22.230
it will release the lease,
and then all the messages

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:23.950
should be sent at that point.

00:31:23.950 --> 00:31:26.160
So this ensures ordered
at least once delivery.

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:27.570
We do have some edge cases
where it could possibly

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:29.069
result in duplicates,
but we figured

00:31:29.069 --> 00:31:33.030
that was better than
having no responses at all.

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:36.287
And this is something we
built over a year ago.

00:31:36.287 --> 00:31:38.120
We haven't had any
problems with it, really.

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:42.697
You have Big Table issues
that are within their SLA,

00:31:42.697 --> 00:31:44.030
so we have blips here and there.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:46.571
It hasn't really impacted users,
and every time we've had one

00:31:46.571 --> 00:31:48.982
of these blips, it's
recovered on its own .

00:31:55.590 --> 00:31:58.110
So conversational platforms
will deliver media

00:31:58.110 --> 00:31:59.310
in different ways.

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:02.490
On the right, you see
an image being uploaded

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:05.180
from Facebook Messenger.

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:07.250
And when that's
sent to our service,

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:10.490
it's in the form of
a short-lived URL.

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:15.320
And other platforms
might deliver this media

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:16.560
in different ways.

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:18.020
So we want to
extract those details

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:19.550
from our back-end services.

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:24.800
So at the edge, in our WebHook,
we record any uploaded media

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:26.420
into GCS.

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:29.917
And then the GCS key is what
we pass around to our back end.

00:32:29.917 --> 00:32:32.000
Once again, it's something
that just works for us.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.220
It was very fast for
us to implement this,

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:36.511
and we haven't had to think
about it since we launched.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:45.260
We built our notification
system with Cloud Data Store.

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:47.860
So this is what proactively
sends messages to users.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:51.580
We handle package tracking
events, deals, Editors' Picks,

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:54.560
and marketing campaigns.

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:57.600
User descriptions are
stored in Datastore.

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:02.170
So the user will subscribe or
unsubscribe to a notification

00:33:02.170 --> 00:33:04.150
through their
conversational interface.

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:07.270
It will be sent to our
notification service, which

00:33:07.270 --> 00:33:10.110
records that in the
notifications DB.

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:13.620
We have Quartz cron jobs
that run periodically

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:16.500
to check to see if any
notifications need to be sent.

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:22.260
It will receive events from eBay
to know when package tracking

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:23.880
updates happened.

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:27.750
And we also call into
our deals API to look up

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:30.720
any new deals for the users.

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:37.290
When this notification system
runs, is invoked by Quartz,

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:41.760
we get a transaction
to essentially lock

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:47.560
the processing of notifications
to a single worker.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:49.900
And this is one area we
actually might want to revisit

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:52.240
our decision to use Data Store.

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:54.790
We used it, at the time,
because it was new,

00:33:54.790 --> 00:33:58.250
and we wanted to see
how it performed.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:01.090
And we've run into rate-limiting
issues with it time and again.

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:03.400
So probably, what
we would do instead

00:34:03.400 --> 00:34:06.284
is use a traditional relational
database or Cloud SQL

00:34:06.284 --> 00:34:07.450
or something else like that.

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:16.560
We built our analytics
system on GCP.

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:18.730
And what you see here is
actually pretty standard.

00:34:18.730 --> 00:34:24.300
Our service will track requests
and responses in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:28.870
We have Cloud Dataflow that does
a stream process of any events,

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:33.210
and it persists the
results into Cloud Storage.

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:36.257
What you see on the right
is a Data Studio dashboard.

00:34:36.257 --> 00:34:36.840
It's not ours.

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:40.000
It's just a sample, just to get
an idea of what it looks like.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:42.540
And when the user
loads the dashboard,

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:45.870
it goes through BigQuery
to load the external data

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:48.090
from Cloud Storage, summarize
it, and then present it

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:48.590
to the user.

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:56.050
So, tying this all
together, I'm going

00:34:56.050 --> 00:34:59.930
to show a video of Google
Assistant in action.

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:03.770
And I didn't go into
localization or multi-region

00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:05.360
support, but just
a heads up, this

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:09.330
is done from the viewpoint
of an Australian user.

00:35:12.739 --> 00:35:14.687
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:35:14.687 --> 00:35:17.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

